No answer leak detected. The problem statement contains only the integral to evaluate.

## Solution

We evaluate the Cauchy principal value
$$I = \text{PV}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{x}{(x^2+4)(x^2-2x+5)}\,dx.$$

**Degree check.** The integrand $f(z)=\dfrac{z}{(z^2+4)(z^2-2z+5)}$ is $O(1/z^3)$ as $|z|\to\infty$, so the integral converges absolutely; the principal value coincides with the ordinary improper integral, and the contribution from a large semicircular arc vanishes.

**Poles.** Factor the denominator:
$$z^2+4=(z-2i)(z+2i),\qquad z^2-2z+5=(z-1-2i)(z-1+2i).$$
All four poles $2i,\,-2i,\,1+2i,\,1-2i$ are simple. The poles in the **upper** half-plane are
$$z_1=2i,\qquad z_2=1+2i.$$

**Contour.** Close the real axis with the upper semicircle $C_R$ of radius $R\to\infty$. Since $|f|=O(1/R^3)$ on $C_R$ and $|C_R|=\pi R$, the arc integral is $O(1/R^2)\to 0$. Hence
$$I = 2\pi i\bigl(\operatorname{Res}_{z=2i}f+\operatorname{Res}_{z=1+2i}f\bigr).$$

**Residue at $z_1=2i$.** Using the fully factored form,
$$\operatorname{Res}_{2i}f=\frac{2i}{(2i+2i)(2i-1-2i)(2i-1+2i)}=\frac{2i}{(4i)(-1)(-1+4i)}=\frac{1}{2(1-4i)}=\frac{1+4i}{34}.$$

**Residue at $z_2=1+2i$.** The vanishing factor is $(z-1-2i)$; the remaining factors evaluated at $1+2i$ are $(z-2i)\to 1$, $(z+2i)\to 1+4i$, $(z-1+2i)\to 4i$. Thus
$$\operatorname{Res}_{1+2i}f=\frac{1+2i}{(1)(1+4i)(4i)}=\frac{1+2i}{4i(1+4i)}=\frac{-2-9i}{68}.$$

**Sum of residues.**
$$\operatorname{Res}_{2i}f+\operatorname{Res}_{1+2i}f=\frac{1+4i}{34}+\frac{-2-9i}{68}=\frac{2+8i-2-9i}{68}=\frac{-i}{68}.$$

**Final value.**
$$I=2\pi i\cdot\frac{-i}{68}=\frac{-2\pi i^2}{68}=\frac{2\pi}{68}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{34}}.$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
